steps.body:
A unique_ptr cannot be copied, but its ownership can *move*. Wrapping the source in `std::move` from `<utility>` lets another unique_ptr take the object over. The heap object stays where it is: only the 8 byte address changes hands, and the source is guaranteed to become `nullptr`. `std::move` itself is only a cast that marks the source as safe to take from, and *Copy and Move Semantics*, later in the course, explains it.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Task {
public:
    explicit Task(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {}
    ~Task() {
        std::cout << "destroyed " << name_ << '\n';
    }
    std::string name() const { return name_; }

private:
    std::string name_;
};

int main() {
    auto inbox = std::make_unique<Task>("report");
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "inbox is " << (inbox ? "full" : "empty") << '\n';
    std::cout << "doing holds " << doing->name() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<Task> doing = std::move(inbox); // inbox is now nullptr
if (inbox == nullptr) { /* or: if (!inbox) */ }
if (doing) { /* true when it owns an object */ }
```

Check before you use `*` or `->`, because dereferencing an empty unique_ptr is undefined behavior.

Ownership moves in a few common ways:

- A factory function returns `std::unique_ptr<T>` by value. Returning a local moves it out automatically.
- A parameter taken by value, as in `void finish(std::unique_ptr<Task> t)`, takes ownership. Call it with `finish(std::move(doing));` as its own statement.
- `p.reset()` deletes the object right now and leaves `p` empty, instead of waiting for the end of scope. Any reference you took earlier with `*p` now dangles.

By the time a call like `finish(std::move(doing));` ends, its by-value parameter has deleted the object it owned. Take a unique_ptr by value only when the function should end up owning the object. Otherwise pass a `T&` or `const T&` and call the function with `*p`.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

class Task {
public:
    explicit Task(const std::string& name) : name_(name) {}
    ~Task() {
        std::cout << "destroyed " << name_ << '\n';
    }
    std::string name() const { return name_; }

private:
    std::string name_;
};

std::unique_ptr<Task> makeTask(const std::string& name) {
    return std::make_unique<Task>(name);
}

void finish(std::unique_ptr<Task> t) {
    std::cout << "finishing " << t->name() << '\n';
}

int main() {
    auto inbox = makeTask("report");
    std::unique_ptr<Task> doing = std::move(inbox);
    std::cout << "inbox is " << (inbox ? "full" : "empty") << '\n';
    if (doing != nullptr) {
        std::cout << "doing holds " << doing->name() << '\n';
    }
    finish(std::move(doing));
    std::cout << "after finish\n";
    std::cout << "doing is " << (doing ? "full" : "empty") << '\n';
    return 0;
}

