steps.body:
Sometimes several parts of a program use the same object and none of them is clearly the last. `std::shared_ptr<T>` handles that. Create one with `std::make_shared<T>(args)`. Unlike a unique_ptr, it can be copied, and every copy is one more owner of the same object.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class Document {
public:
    explicit Document(const std::string& title) : title_(title) {}
    ~Document() {
        std::cout << title_ << " deleted\n";
    }
    std::string title() const { return title_; }

private:
    std::string title_;
};

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
auto doc = std::make_shared<Document>("notes");
std::cout << doc.use_count() << '\n';  // 1
auto editor = doc;
std::cout << doc.use_count() << '\n';  // 2
editor.reset();
std::cout << doc.use_count() << '\n';  // 1 again
```

A shared_ptr is 16 bytes: the object's address plus the address of a control block that holds the owner count. `make_shared` puts the object and that block in one heap allocation. A copy adds 1 to the count, and a destroyed or reset owner subtracts 1. When the count reaches 0, the last owner deletes the object. `use_count()` reports the count, and it is 0 for an empty shared_ptr.

Two things to know:

- If two objects hold shared_ptrs to each other, neither count ever reaches 0 and both leak. Make one side a `std::weak_ptr<T>`, which watches the object without owning it. The next part shows how.
- Shared ownership costs a control block and a count update on every copy, and it makes lifetimes harder to follow. Prefer unique_ptr by default and use shared_ptr only when ownership is truly shared.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

class Document {
public:
    explicit Document(const std::string& title) : title_(title) {}
    ~Document() {
        std::cout << title_ << " deleted\n";
    }
    std::string title() const { return title_; }

private:
    std::string title_;
};

int main() {
    std::shared_ptr<Document> archive;
    {
        auto doc = std::make_shared<Document>("notes");
        std::cout << "owners " << doc.use_count() << '\n';
        {
            auto editor = doc;
            std::cout << "owners " << doc.use_count() << '\n';
            std::cout << "editing " << editor->title() << '\n';
        }
        std::cout << "owners " << doc.use_count() << '\n';
        archive = doc;
        std::cout << "owners " << doc.use_count() << '\n';
    }
    std::cout << "owners " << archive.use_count() << '\n';
    archive.reset();
    std::cout << "owners " << archive.use_count() << '\n';
    std::cout << "end of main\n";
    return 0;
}

