steps.body:
A folder tree is a common shape: each folder owns its subfolders, and each subfolder needs to reach its parent. Make both links shared_ptrs and you build a *cycle*:

```cpp
struct Folder {
    explicit Folder(const std::string& n) : name(n) {}
    ~Folder() { std::cout << name << " deleted\n"; }
    std::string name;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Folder>> children;
    std::shared_ptr<Folder> parent;  // owns its parent too: a cycle
};
{
    auto home = std::make_shared<Folder>("home");
    auto docs = std::make_shared<Folder>("docs");
    home->children.push_back(docs);
    docs->parent = home;
    std::cout << home.use_count() << ' ' << docs.use_count() << '\n';  // 2 2
}
std::cout << "end of main\n";  // and no "deleted" line ever prints
```

At the closing brace each count drops to 1, held by the other folder, so neither destructor runs. Both objects *leak*: they stay on the heap with nothing left to delete them, and nothing warns you.

`std::weak_ptr<T>` from `<memory>` breaks the cycle. Assigned a shared_ptr, it points at the same object without owning it, so `use_count()` doesn't change. The object can die while it watches, so a weak_ptr has no `->` or `*`. Its `lock()` returns a shared_ptr, an owner while you keep it, or an empty one once the object is gone, and `expired()` says whether it's gone. Declared in an `if` condition, the result is tested like `if (p)`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

struct Folder {
    explicit Folder(const std::string& n) : name(n) {}
    std::string name;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Folder>> children;
    std::weak_ptr<Folder> parent;
};

int main() {
    auto home = std::make_shared<Folder>("home");
    auto docs = std::make_shared<Folder>("docs");
    home->children.push_back(docs);
    docs->parent = home;
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (auto p = docs->parent.lock()) {  // a shared_ptr, empty if home is gone
    std::cout << p->name << " owners " << p.use_count() << '\n';  // home owners 2
}
```

Underneath, the control block also counts weak_ptrs, and it stays until the last one is gone, so `lock()` can still find the owner count at 0. With `make_shared` the object's memory sits in that block, so it's freed only then.

The rule: owners point down, and anything that points back or only observes holds a weak_ptr. That covers child to parent links, observers (listeners a sender mustn't keep alive, the observer pattern of *Design Patterns in C++*), and caches, like a `std::map<std::string, std::weak_ptr<Image>>` that shares an image while someone uses it and loads it again once it's gone.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Folder {
    explicit Folder(const std::string& n) : name(n) {}
    ~Folder() {
        std::cout << name << " deleted\n";
    }
    std::string name;
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<Folder>> children;  // owns its subfolders
    std::weak_ptr<Folder> parent;                    // only watches its parent
};

void printPath(const Folder& f) {
    if (auto p = f.parent.lock()) {
        std::cout << p->name << '/' << f.name << " (parent owners " << p.use_count() << ")\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << f.name << " has no parent\n";
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::weak_ptr<Folder> watch;
    {
        auto home = std::make_shared<Folder>("home");
        auto docs = std::make_shared<Folder>("docs");
        home->children.push_back(docs);
        docs->parent = home;
        std::cout << "home owners " << home.use_count() << '\n';
        std::cout << "docs owners " << docs.use_count() << '\n';
        printPath(*docs);
        printPath(*home);
        watch = home;
        std::cout << "watch expired " << watch.expired() << '\n';
        std::cout << "leaving block\n";
    }
    std::cout << "watch expired " << watch.expired() << '\n';
    if (!watch.lock()) {
        std::cout << "home is gone\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

